Windows-only companion to emulator-patches/lockstep-headless.patch.

On Windows the emulator links with mingw g++ against whatever SDL2 the machine
has, typically scoop's MSVC build. That package's SDL2main.lib supplies its own
`main`, which collides with mingw's C runtime ("multiple definition of `main`").
sim/Makefile therefore builds this tree with -DSDL_MAIN_HANDLED and without
-lSDL2main, which leaves SDL expecting the application to announce its entry
point itself.

An SDL initialisation detail only: it changes nothing about emulation, which
the byte-identical lockstep_blit_scene comparison confirms.

Applied after lockstep-headless.patch, so the context below includes that
patch's lockstep_init() call.

--- a/src/gte.cpp
+++ b/src/gte.cpp
@@ -1401,6 +1401,11 @@
 }
 
 int main(int argC, char* argV[]) {
+#ifdef SDL_MAIN_HANDLED
+    // We keep our own main (see the Windows branch in sim/Makefile), so
+    // tell SDL the entry-point handshake is already taken care of.
+    SDL_SetMainReady();
+#endif
 	lockstep_init();
 	srand(time(NULL));
 	cartridge_state.rom = new uint8_t[1 << 21];
